1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,919
Lists proudly allow duplicates and
remember order.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:14,839
The next product requirement often flips
that pride into a bug: a user should have
each role only once, a crawler should not
visit the same url twice, a tag cloud
should not print "java" three times.

3
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:23,200
Suppose you store roles in a list and
check membership with contains before
every add. It works for tiny data and
becomes awkward as rules grow.

4
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:39,560
The natural question is: is there a
collection whose whole point is
uniqueness? That collection is a Set. Set
means uniqueness — and uniqueness means
equals/hashCode discipline. Look at this
java for a moment. Walk it.

5
00:00:39,679 --> 00:00:51,920
The first add inserts "a". The second add
sees an equal element already present and
leaves the set unchanged. Size stays one.
If you needed two entries, you wanted a
list.

6
00:00:52,039 --> 00:01:02,719
Under a HashSet, uniqueness is decided by
hashing into buckets, then confirming with
equals. If two objects are equal, they
must share a hash code.

7
00:01:02,839 --> 00:01:13,560
If you break that contract, a set can hold
what looks like duplicates, or fail to
find what you just inserted. For your own
classes, implementing equals and hashCode
together is not optional decoration.

8
00:01:13,679 --> 00:01:24,399
Different set implementations trade
properties: Look at this java for a
moment. HashSet is the common default when
you only care about membership.
LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order.

9
00:01:24,519 --> 00:01:32,239
TreeSet keeps elements sorted, which means
it needs a consistent ordering — natural
Comparable order or an explicit
Comparator.

10
00:01:32,359 --> 00:01:40,480
If the comparator says two elements are
equal for ordering purposes, the set
treats them as duplicates even if equals
would disagree.

11
00:01:40,599 --> 00:01:52,679
Here is a domain-shaped example where set
algebra shows up in real rules: Look at
this java for a moment. removeAll,
retainAll, and addAll express difference,
intersection, and union.

12
00:01:52,799 --> 00:02:05,879
Permission checks and feature flag merges
sound like set talk once you stop forcing
lists to pretend. One sharp edge: do not
mutate fields used in hashing while an
object sits in a set. Look at this java
for a moment.

13
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:18,919
After mutation, the object may live in the
wrong bucket. Treat elements as
effectively immutable while they are set
members, or remove, mutate, and re-add.
What if we skip sets and keep unique lists
by hand?

14
00:02:19,099 --> 00:02:31,539
Fine for one call site. Then five call
sites forget the check. A set makes the
rule structural instead of ceremonial.
Equality discipline with a domain type:
Look at this java for a moment.

15
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:42,500
Because Role is a record, equal names
compare equal and hash alike. A hand-
written class that compares names in
equals but forgets name in hashCode would
break HashSet membership.

16
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:48,819
Iteration order stories cause flaky tests.
A HashSet may print elements in different
orders across runs.

17
00:02:48,939 --> 00:03:04,219
If a test asserts a stringified set,
prefer LinkedHashSet, a sorted set, or
compare as sets without relying on order.
Lists allowed duplicates; many domains
forbid them. Sets answered with uniqueness
via equals/hashCode.

18
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:17,099
HashSet, LinkedHashSet, and TreeSet traded
speed, insertion order, and sorted order.
Mutating hashed fields showed a classic
foot-gun. Uniqueness of elements is still
not lookup by key.

19
00:03:17,219 --> 00:03:31,099
Often you need "given a user id, find the
user" or "given a sku, find the price."
Searching a list linearly gets old fast.
How does Java give us keyed lookup? That
is the pressure that brings maps.
